An early console for Cooperative Linux.

The guest has no device to talk to -- it runs inside a host process's address
space and its only channel out is memory both sides can see. So the console
appends bytes to a ring in the passage page and the host drains it after the
next world switch.

co_colinux_console_ring is filled in by the host *before* the guest executes
anything, which is the point: output works from the first instruction rather
than from whenever setup_early_printk() runs during setup_arch(). A real
machine must bootstrap its own console and is blind until it does. Being blind
through early boot is how a port like this gets stuck.

`written` counts everything the guest tried to emit, including what did not
fit, so the host can report truncation instead of silently showing a short log.

--- a/arch/x86/kernel/early_printk.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/early_printk.c
@@ -370,6 +370,57 @@
 	.index =	-1,
